# cargo-fuzz build output.
target/
# Its own lockfile: this crate exists to be rebuilt against whatever the main crate
# resolves, and a pinned one here only ever goes stale.
Cargo.lock

# Crash inputs libFuzzer writes when a target dies. Deliberately NOT committed as
# artefacts: a crash is a defect, so the input belongs in a regression test under
# `tests/` and in `corpus/<target>/`, not in a directory nothing reads.
artifacts/

# What is committed under `corpus/` is the hand-written seed set — valid messages for
# each protocol, so the fuzzer starts from the shapes the decoders accept instead of
# spending its budget rediscovering headers. libFuzzer also *writes* every
# coverage-expanding input it finds back into these directories, and those are not
# committed: they are thousands of files, they are reproducible from the seeds, and CI
# uploads the grown corpus as a build artefact instead. Re-seed with
# `git clean -fd corpus/ && git checkout corpus/` after a local run.
